{"text": "AMERICAN SOCIETY OF HEATING AND AIR-CONDITIONING ENGINEERS, Inc.\nNEW YORK 13, N. \u00a5.\n\u2018Reply to\nReszancn Lanonatory\n7218 vou Ave. Onnvmxanp 8, Oro\n\nTELEPHONE UTAH 1-6161\nPROGRAM OF RESEARCH ON ODORS\nBy E. R. Keiser, Director of Research\n\nand\n\nP. Kerka, Research Engineer\n\nApril 25, 1955\n\nTHE PROBLEM:\n\nodors in indoor spaces are a common nuisance. Tobacco, body, cook\ning, exid process odors are the chief offenders. Odors contribute to nauses\n\u2018and discomfort. Continuous exposure to odors is known to cause fatigue and\na general decline of awareness. Where odors persist, business suffers, and\nthe public in inconvenienced and annoyed.\n\nUnder some conditions air cooling equipment accumulates odorous de~\nposits, which are reliberated under other conditions to cause the so-called\n\"air-conditioning odor\". Odors are adsorbed on indoor building surfaces,\nrugs, and drapes, and are reliberated over long periods of time.\n\nThe ventilation of building spaces with large quantities of outside\nair for the purpose of dispersing odors has several limitations. The out-\nside air in cities frequently contains odors and appreciable dust. \u2018The\nair must be heated and hmidified, or cooled and dehunidified at consider-\nable expense. Because of normal infiltration into buildings, little or no\nadditionel outdoor air may be required to supply oxygen for breathing, or\nto remove CO, from exhaled air.\n\n\u2018The human nose is the only known means for sensing odor intensity,\nput it is affected by nmerous factors and its reliability for scientific\nodor studies needs further investigation. \u2018he effect of air temperature\n\u2018and humidity, concentration of odorant, and time of exposure, on intensity\nof odor perception needs to be established.\n\nPURPOSE AND OBJECTIVE:\n\n\u2018The purpose of the program is to establish an engineering basis for\nthe control of odors in air-conditioning, to the end that air systems can\nbe designed for greater certainty of satisfactory results, and thermal econo-\nny.\n\nFundamental data on odor liberation, perception, and adsorption or\ndestruction are necessary to understand and cope with the phenomena of odors",
